The release workflow copies `misaka-studiod` (or `.exe`) here before `cargo tauri build`, and the
bundle's `resources: ["resources/*"]` carries it into the installer — which is what lets an
installed app run with no Rust, no Node and no PATH entry on the user's machine.

This file exists so the directory does, because a resource glob that matches nothing is behaviour
this repository cannot test on the platform that matters: the Windows bundle is only ever built by
CI. Keeping the directory present means the glob always has this file to match, so the local
`cargo tauri build` path does not depend on an assumption nobody here can check.
